Problems from [LB16]: 6.1 problem 4 and 6.2 problems 1 and 2 parts a) b) and c)</p>

<exercise number="">
    <title>Question 1.A</title>
    <statement>Write a program in Matlab that would implement Euler's Method. The function should have the structure: [approx] = euler(myfun,t0,y0,dt,n) where myfun is an anonymous function for <m> \frac {dy}{dt}=f(t,y)</m>, t0 is the initial time value, y0 is the initial y value, dt is the step size, and n is the number of steps.   </statement>
    <solution>See example on D2L of euler.m </solution>
    </exercise>

<exercise number=""> <title>Question 1.B</title>
<statement>Write a program in Matlab that would implement Modified Euler's Method with a similar structure as the program in Question 1.A. </statement></exercise>

<exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.C</title>
        <statement>
            <p>For each of the following, calculate the absolute error between Euler's Method and the analytical answer for: <m>\Delta t =0.1</m> for ten steps, <m>\Delta t =0.05</m> for 20 steps, and <m>\Delta t = 0.01</m> for 100 steps. Repeat the same calculations using Modified Euler.</p>
            <ul marker="">
                <li><p>a) <m>y' = t-\sin(t)</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=3</m></p></li>
                <li><p>b) <m>y' = t-y</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=4</m></p></li>
                <li><p>c) <m>y' = e^{-t}-2y</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=5</m></p></li>
                <li><p>d) <m>y' = 2yt</m>; <m>\quad y(0)=-2</m></p></li>
                <li><p>e) <m>y' = e^{-t}-y</m>; <m>\quad y(1)=2e</m></p></li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>For part b), using integration factors we can solve and get the analytical solution of <m>y=t-1+5e^{-t}</m>.</p>
            <console>
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> [approx] = euler(@(t,y) t-y,0,4,0.1,10);
                >> soln = @(t) t-1+5*exp(-t);
                >> abs(soln(1)-approx)
                ]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[
                ans = 0.0960
                ]]></output>
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> [approx] = euler(@(t,y) t-y,0,4,0.05,20);
                >> abs(soln(1)-approx)
                ]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[
                ans = 0.0470
                ]]></output>
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> [approx] = euler(@(t,y) t-y,0,4,0.01,100);
                >> abs(soln(1)-approx)
                ]]></input>
                <output><![CDATA[
                ans = 0.0092
                ]]></output>
            </console>
            <p>We see that Modified Euler will converge faster with the following results:</p>
            <p>
                <ul marker="disc">
                    <li><p><m>\Delta t = 0.1 \implies \text{abs}(soln(1)-approx) = 0.0033</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\Delta t = 0.05 \implies \text{abs}(soln(1)-approx) = 7.9590\times 10^{-4}</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>\Delta t = 0.01 \implies \text{abs}(soln(1)-approx) = 3.0888\times 10^{-5}</m></p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Describe how the results in Question 1.C reveal the fact that Euler's Method is first order and Modified Euler's is second order.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.E</title>
        <statement>
            <p>For the initial value problem, <m>\frac{dy}{dt} = t^2-y^2</m> with <m>y(0)=1</m>. Derive the formula for the second order Taylor's Method (as described in section 6.2 of the text).</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                For this portion, keep in mind that <m>y'=f(t,y) = t^2-y^2</m>. It follows the second order Taylor's expansion takes the form:
                <me>y_{\text{new}} = y + h y' + \frac{h^2}{2} y'',</me>
                but since <m>y' = f(t,y) = t^2 -y^2</m> it follows that:
                <me>y_{\text{new}} = y + h f(t,y) + \frac{h^2}{2} \frac{d}{dt}f(t,y).</me>
                The chain rule produces the fact that <m>\frac{d}{dt}f(t,y) = f_t + f_y y'</m> and so it follows that:
                <md>
                    <mrow>y_{\text{new}} \amp= y + h f(t,y) + \frac{h^2}{2} \frac{d}{dt}f(t,y)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= y + h (t^2-y^2) + \frac{h^2}{2} (2t -2y(t^2-y^2)).</mrow>
                </md>
                The final expression is used for second order Taylor's for the given initial value problem.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

