    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.H</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall the pendulum model from section 6.1:
                <me>\frac{d^2 \theta}{dt^2} + \left(\frac{c}{m}\right)\frac{d\theta}{dt} + \frac{g}{\ell} \sin(\theta) = 0,</me>
                if it is given <m>\theta(0)=2.2</m> and <m>\frac{d\theta}{dt}(0)=-1.1</m> then use this information to convert this second order differential equation to a first order system. (Please also convert the initial value information into the first order system.)
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Let <m>z = \frac{d\theta}{dt}</m> then the second order problem turns into the following first order system:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{d\theta}{dt} \amp= z, \quad \theta(0) = 2.2</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dz}{dt} \amp= \frac{-cz}{m} - \frac{g}{\ell} \sin(\theta), \quad z(0)=-1.1</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 1.J</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Use Modified Euler's to approximate the solution to <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=t-y^2</m> with <m>y(0)=1</m>. Approximate the value at t=2 using a step size of <m>\Delta t=0.1</m> and compare the results with a step size of <m>\Delta t=0.05</m>. Does this have an analytical solution?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>See example on D2L of <c>modified_euler.m</c>. This problem cannot be separated and it is not linear, which means the two techniques we have been using to solve first order equations will not work on this problem. The existence and uniqueness theorem does guarantee that we have a unique solution to this initial value problem, but we lack the tools to find the analytical solution.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

