            <p>
                <alert>Section 6.4 of [LB16] -  Adaptive Methods</alert>
            </p>
      
    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.A</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Outline the premise of an adaptive method. Are adaptive methods more accurate than the methods that have fixed step sizes? Please explain your answer.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Adaptive methods are built for efficiency, any fixed point method can be just as accurate or more accurate with suitably small step sizes.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.B</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Describe the potential problems when using an adaptive method.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Adaptive methods can run into problems with singularities just as we observed with adaptive integration. If the true solution has a vertical asymptote then adaptive methods will likely have a problem rendering a solution. For simple problems, the large investment in setting up an adaptive method might not be worth the return on efficiency.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.C</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A simple adaptive method in Matlab is the <c>ode23</c> algorithm. The format for the <c>ode23</c> program is: <c>[t,y] = ode23(odefun,tspan,y0)</c>, where <c>odefun</c> is the function for the ODE, <c>tspan</c> is a vector that contains the starting and stopping value of t, and <c>y0</c> is the initial y value. Consider one of our favorite examples: <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=t-y</m> with <m>y(0)=4</m> and we are interested in the value at t=2. The code in Matlab to use <c>ode23</c> to approximate a solution to this problem is:</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> [t,y] = ode23(@(t,y) t-y,[0 2],4);    
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>Please use this information to use Matlab and <c>ode23</c> to solve <m>\frac{dy}{dt}=t-y^2</m> with <m>y(0)=4</m> and we are interested in the value at t=2. Give a plot of your solution.</p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Matlab has several solvers for initial value problems with another example being <c>ode45</c>. A person new to the subject might think <c>ode45</c> represents <m>45^{\text{th}}</m> order method, which is definitely not the case. Based on what you know about adaptive methods, please describe what <c>ode45</c> might represent as a numerical solver for initial value problems.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>An adaptive method wtih the label of 45 would translate into predictor method that is fourth order and a corrector method that is fifth order. Of course, adpative methods have evolved to be far more complicated than a simple predictor / corrector construct, but the origins do trace back to the simple predictor / corrector methods. 
            </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 4.E</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Consider the initial value problem of <m>\frac{dy}{dt} = y</m> with <m>y(1) = 4</m>. In this case, the function, <m>f(t,y)</m> is really just a function of <m>y</m>. Does this remove the ability of numerical methods to solve the problem? How does the slope field change with this type of problem?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The fact that <m>t</m> is not part of <m>f(t,y)</m> is not an obstacle to solving the problem. This type of problem is labeled an autonomous system in differential equations since it does not explicitly depend on <m>t</m>. The slope field for this type of problem has the feature that slopes do not depend on <m>t</m>, which means the slope is not changed as you move horizontally (in the direction of <m>t</m>) in the slope field.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
