    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 5.D</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Suppose that for an initial value problem the function f(t,y) is not continuous on an open interval that contains the initial value. Does this mean there is not a solution? Please explain your answer.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The theorem is one way and so if f meets the criteria of the theorem then we are guaranteed there is a unique solution. If the f(t,y) does not meet the criteria then the theorem does not apply and there could be a solution or there might not be a solution (or an infinite number of solutions).</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 5.E</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Create a Matlab program that will use Euler's Method to solve the differential equation:
                <me>\frac{dy}{dt} = -20y, \quad y(0)=1,</me>
                running solutions out to t=2. Please use the step sizes of dt = 0.01, 0.05, and 0.1. Compare these estimates to the exact solution.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>This example illustrates the feature of stability when using a numerical method for solving a differential equation. In this case, approximations behave well with the smallest step size, but approximations have abnormally large errors for the larger step sizes. In some cases, methods have conditional stability where solutions are stable for step sizes that are in a fixed range of values.</p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

