    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 6.F</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that for equations of one variable we defined absolute error as:
                <me>\text{Absolute Error}=|y(t_n)-y_n|</me>
                and we defined absolute relative error as:
                <me>\text{Absolute Relative Error} = \frac{|y(t_n)-y_n|}{|y(t_n)|},</me>
                where <m>y(t_n)</m> is the exact answer at <m>t_n</m> and <m>y_n</m> is the approximation corresponding to <m>t_n</m>. All of this theory translates into systems, if we define <m>\vec{w}_n</m> as the approximation to a system problem at the nth step and let <m>\vec{w}(t_n)</m> represent the exact solution at the given time step then explain why the definitions should be:
                <me>\text{Absolute Error}=\Vert \vec{w}(t_n)-\vec{w}_n\Vert</me>
                and absolute relative error as:
                <me>\text{Absolute Relative Error} = \frac{\Vert \vec{w}(t_n)-\vec{w}_n\Vert}{\Vert \vec{w}(t_n)\Vert},</me>
                and provide the definition of <m>\Vert \vec{x} \Vert</m> for a vector with $n$ entries.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The answer is rather simple, we swap the use of the absolute value <m>|y|</m> to the use of the vector norm <m>\Vert \vec{w} \Vert</m>. To be clear, the vector norm is the Euclidean length calculation:
                <me>\Biggr \Vert \begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{pmatrix} \Biggr \Vert = \sqrt{a^2+b^2+c^2}.</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise number="">
        <title>Question 6.G</title>
        <statement>
            <p>If we had an approximation to a system problem that is given by
                <me>\vec{w}_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 1.1 \\ -2.3 \\ 1.46 \end{pmatrix}</me>
                and the exact answer is given by
                <me>\vec{w}(t_3) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -2.5 \\ 1.5 \end{pmatrix}.</me>
                Consider the following Matlab code:
            </p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> approx = [1.1; -2.3; 1.46];
                >> exact = [1; -2.5; 1.5];
                >> norm(exact - approx)
                ans = 0.2272  
                ]]></input>
            </program>
            <p>Explain what is being calculated in the Matlab code and how it relates to Question 6.F. Please be as complete as possible and use this information to calculate the absolute relative error.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The Matlab code of <c>norm(a-b)</c> is the Matlab command that calculates <m>\Vert \vec{a} - \vec{b} \Vert</m> and so the code given above is calculating the absolute error. If there is an interest in calculating the absolute relative error then that can be accomplished via:</p>
            <program language="matlab">
                <input><![CDATA[
                >> approx = [1.1; -2.3; 1.46];
                >> exact = [1; -2.5; 1.5];
                >> norm(exact - approx)/norm(exact)
                ans =    0.0737   
                ]]></input>
            </program>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

